{"chunk_id": "ecfb1f485310", "text": "In this moral tale recounted by Bhishma, a pigeon offers shelter to a freezing fowler\u2014the very hunter who kills birds like him. The pigeon insists that hospitality must be given even to an enemy who seeks refuge, comparing it to a tree that does not deny shade even to the man who cuts it down. After warming the fowler, the pigeon realizes he has no food to offer and, believing a host must feed a guest, sacrifices himself by entering the fire. Witnessing this extraordinary act of selfless duty, the fowler is overcome with remorse. He renounces his cruel profession, frees the captured she\u2011pigeon, and embraces a life of penance. The grieving she\u2011pigeon later joins her husband in the fire, and both are rewarded in heaven, illustrating the power of hospitality, sacrifice, and moral awakening.\nA pigeon sacrifices himself to feed a freezing fowler guest; the hunter repents, renounces violence, and the pigeons attain heaven through supreme hospitality and devotion.\nThemes: hospitality as sacred duty, self-sacrifice, repentance and moral transformation, marital devotion, righteousness (dharma)", "vector": [0.001073631108738482, -0.029597127810120583, -0.002137236064299941, 0.011991248466074467, -0.017512302845716476, 0.02641550451517105, -0.008662573993206024, -0.000721881166100502, -0.030532900243997574, -0.026749707758426666, 0.01672358065843582, 0.03676246851682663, 0.01027343887835741, -0.0021305521950125694, -0.0009900800650939345, -0.007365861441940069, 0.04061250016093254, -0.015279818326234818, 0.016322536394000053, -0.02277936227619648, -0.015373395755887032, 0.021696539595723152, -0.013221120461821556, -0.0013059030752629042, 0.004053897690027952, 0.021696539595723152, 0.017859874293208122, -0.021014763042330742, 0.017485566437244415, 0.0031548880506306887, 0.0006408366607502103, 0.0007009934051893651, -0.0006412544171325862, -0.01986510120332241, -0.01921006105840206, -0.002048671944066882, -0.0089834099635481